    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d231">

                <pb facs="0384" n="296" sameAs="frus:frus1892/pg296"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Herbert to Mr. Foster.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >British Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Newport, R.
                                I.</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1892-08-06"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">August 6, 1892</hi></date>. (Received
                        August 8.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Foster</hi>: I have just received a telegram
                    from Sir John Abbott, the prime minister of the Dominion, in which he informs me
                    that the canal toll question will receive the further consideration of the
                    Canadian Government. The members of the cabinet are, however, scattered all over
                    the country, Sir John himself having only returned, two days ago from, a
                    fortnight’s stay up the Restigouche; and, as so grave a question will
                    necessitate a full meeting of the cabinet, he fears that it will be impossible
                    to arrive at a final decision for a week or ten days longer.</p>
                <p>While much regretting this delay, I venture to express the hope that no further
                    action will be taken by the United States Government in the matter, until I
                    shall have received the answer of the Canadian Government.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Michael H.
                    Herbert.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
